4. ANSWER (natural-language hypothesis)
==============================================================================
In this replication-project sample (table1 study_data, n=927 rows / 141 unique O/R pairs), original-paper length is measured by the field `length` (pages of the ORIGINAL paper) and the two categories are defined solely by the `project` key: project=='ee' = Experimental Economics (n=18, identical to discipline=='Economics') and project=='rpp' = Psychology (n=97). Experimental Economics original papers average 28.0 pages (median 27.5, SD 7.4, IQR 23.5-30.0, range 16-45), whereas Psychology (rpp) original papers average 11.22 pages (median 9, SD 6.3, IQR 7-16, range 1-31, right-skewed). The gap is 16.78 pages, i.e. econ originals are 2.50x as long (bootstrap 95% CI for the difference 13.3-20.4 pages, for the ratio 2.1-2.9; Welch t=9.06, p=7.4e-09; Mann-Whitney U=1682, p=4.5e-10). Interpretation is bounded by cross-disciplinary comparability: all 18 ee papers are AER/QJE articles whose conventions (figures, tables, appendices) and the psychology journals used by rpp (including 3-page Psychological Science reports) differ, so this gap partly reflects journal/discipline length norms rather than research scale or quality on a common scale, and it must not be read as a general claim about the two fields.

